    Mathematical Institute, University of Oxford, United Kingdom
  </author-affiliation>>>|<doc-date|<date|>>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    We introduce Wilson\UItô diffusions, a class of random fields on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> that change continuously along a scale parameter
    via a Markovian dynamics with <with|font-shape|italic|local>
    coefficients. Described via forward-backward stochastic differential
    equations, their observables naturally form a pre-factorization algebra
    <with|font-shape|italic|à la> Costello--Gwilliam. We argue that this is a
    new non-perturbative quantization method applicable also to gauge
    theories and independent of a path-integral formulation. Whenever a
    path-integral is available, this approach reproduces the setting of
    Wilson--Polchinski flow equations.
  </abstract>>

  <section|Introduction>

  The Kadanoff\UWilson point of view on quantum field theory is a central
  idea in modern physics. It dictates that one should develop the fluctuation
  of quantum or statistical fields along a scale decomposition associated to
  the tunable precision of the observation device. In this framework a random
  Euclidean field is described by a stochastic process
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> where <math|a>
  parametrizes a scale of observation with characteristic length <math|1/a>
  and <math|\<varphi\><rsub|a>> is the corresponding observation of the
  field, which must be thought of as containing fluctuations with spatial
  scales <math|\<gtrsim\>1/a>. It is natural to assume that, as we gradually
  increase the resolution of our measuring devices, the resulting
  measurements vary continuously. Therefore we will postulate that the
  stochastic process <math|\<varphi\><rsub|a>> is a <em|pathwise> continuous
  function of <math|a>. It is also reasonable to assume that
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|a>> contains all the information gathered by
  observations with lesser precisions, which implies that it is a Markov
  process along the scale parameter, and we assume further that
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|a>\<rightarrow\>\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> as
  <math|a\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>. We denote by
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>=<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
  the filtration generated by <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
  and by <math|<bbE><rsub|b><around|[|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|]>>
  the operator of conditional expectation given
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F><rsub|b>>, for any <math|b\<geqslant\>0>, with
  <math|\<bbb-E\>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>> the full expectation. We assume that
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|0>=0> whenever the fields take value in a vector
  space; otherwise we take <math|\<varphi\><rsub|0>> to be a fixed default
  (classical) configuration (e.g. a background field). We say that a
  functional <math|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>F<around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>> of the
  field <math|\<varphi\>> is supported in
  <math|U\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> if
  <math|F<around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>=F<around*|(|\<psi\>|)>> for any field
  <math|\<psi\>> such that <math|\<varphi\>=\<psi\>> in <math|U>. For
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> let <math|U<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<assign\><around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>:d<around*|(|x,U|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>|}>>
  be the <math|\<varepsilon\>>-enlargement of <math|U>.

  <\definition>
    <label|def:obs>An <with|font-series|bold|observable> <math|O> is a
    stochastic process <math|<around|(|O<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
    which is an <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>>-martingale, i.e.

    <\equation*>
      O<rsub|b>=<bbE><rsub|b><around|[|O<rsub|a>|]>,<space|2em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>a\<less\>\<infty\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    An <with|font-series|bold|observable> <math|O> is said to be
    <with|font-series|bold|supported on> <math|U> if there exists a family of
    functionals <math|<around*|(|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|a>|(>\<cdot\><around*|)||)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
    each supported on the <math|a<rsup|-1>>-enlargement
    <math|U<rsub|a<rsup|-1>>> and such that
    <math|R<rsub|a><rsup|O>\<assign\>O<rsub|a>-<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>\<rightarrow\>0>
    as <math|a\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>. We call such
    <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>> a <with|font-series|bold|germ for
    <math|O>>. A <with|font-series|bold|local observable (field)> is field of
    observables <math|x\<mapsto\>O<around|(|x|)>> such that the observable
    <math|O<around|(|x|)>> is supported on the singleton
    <math|<around|{|x|}>> for all <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>.
  </definition>

  The definition of the <math|a<rsup|-1>>-enlargement is a matter of
  convention. An observable <math|O> that has a germ

  <\equation*>
    <wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-O\><rsub|a><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>,\<ldots\>,\<nabla\><rsup|k>*\<varphi\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  for some finite <math|k> and some function <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|a>>, is
  local. Germs serve to parametrize the space of local observables via
  concrete functionals of the fields.

  One owes to <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|Itô>> the
  fundamental insight that continuous diffusions can be constructed via
  stochastic differential equations <cite|stroockMarkovProcessesIto2003>. As
  such, since we postulated that the scale description
  <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> of a random field is a
  \ continuous Markov process, it is completely described via its
  infinitesimal rate of change

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>=\<varphi\><rsub|a+\<mathd\>*a>-\<varphi\><rsub|a>=B<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>\<mathd\>a+\<mathd\>M<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>
  </equation*>

  which comes in two parts: a <with|font-shape|italic|drift>
  <math|B<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>*\<mathd\>a> and a
  <with|font-shape|italic|martingale> part
  <math|\<mathd\>M<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>>. The drift component
  <math|B<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>> is the part of the rate of change which
  can be predicted from the large scale observations, while the
  \Pinnovation\Q process <math|M> is a martingale w.r.t. this filtration, it
  models the additional information gained by augmenting the resolution.
  Recall a continuous martingale is described via its quadratic variation
  process <math|<around|\<langle\>|M|\<rangle\>><rsub|a>><nbsp><cite|Rogers_Williams_2000>.

  Enters <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|Wilson>>. We inject
  in this standard framework the spatial random structure of Euclidean
  quantum fields by imposing that the dynamics is completely described by
  \<#2018\><with|font-shape|italic|local>\<#2019\> coefficients. We do so by
  postulating that there exists a local observable field
  <math|<around|(|f<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> which models the
  <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|microscopic force>> and
  that, inspired by the Kadanoff\UWilson block averaging procedure, the drift
  <math|B<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>> at scale <math|a> is determined by an
  averaging of the force field <math|f<rsub|a>> over a region of size
  <math|1/a>. Therefore we introduce a \Pblock averaging\Q operator
  <math|C<rsub|a>=C<rsub|a><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>>, which could be
  random and depend on <math|\<varphi\><rsub|a>>. We require <math|C<rsub|a>>
  to have support on a ball of radius <math|1/a>, that
  <math|a\<mapsto\>C<rsub|a><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>> varies smoothly for all
  <math|\<psi\>>, that <math|C<rsub|a>> be symmetric and positive definite
  and such that <math|C<rsub|\<infty\>>=1>. For example, one can take

  <\equation>
    <around*|(|C<rsub|a>h|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int>a<rsup|d
    >\<chi\><around*|(|<around*|(|x-y|)>a|)>h<around*|(|y|)>\<mathd\>y,<label|eq:main-averaging>
  </equation>

  where <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> and <math|\<chi\>> is a smooth,
  radially symmetric, positive definite function of unit integral. In
  particular note that <math|C<rsub|0>=0> on all sufficiently nice functions
  <math|h>. Since the drift will be integrated along the scales, the local
  averaging has to be done in such a way as to not over-count the
  contributions of the microscopic force. We denote by
  <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|a>C<rsub|a>> the
  scale-derivative of this averaging and let
  <math|B<rsup|\<varphi\>><rsub|a>\<assign\><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a>>.
  This fixes the previsible part of the stochastic dynamics as a function of
  the microscopic force. To complete our description we need to specify also
  the quadratic variation of the martingale part. Using the same principles
  we can assume\ 

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|M|\<rangle\>><rsub|a>\<assign\><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2><rsub|a>\<sigma\><rsup|2><rsub|a>
    <wide|C|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2><rsub|a>*\<mathd\>a
  </equation*>

  for a microscopic positive \<#2018\>\<#2018\>diffusivity\Q
  <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsup|2><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> which is a local
  observable field of positive scalars. In this way the local diffusion on
  scales is determined by the datum of two local observable fields
  <math|<around|(|f<rsub|a>,\<sigma\><rsup|2><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> and a
  family of averaging operators <math|<around|(|C<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>>. In
  particular, by standard results there exists, possibly on an extended
  probability space, a cylindrical <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>>-Brownian
  motion\ 

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><around*|[|W<rsub|b><around*|(|x|)>W<rsub|a><around*|(|y|)>|]>=<around*|(|b\<wedge\>a|)>*\<delta\><around*|(|x-y|)>,
  </equation*>

  such that

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|a>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2><rsub|b>*\<sigma\><rsub|b>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|b>
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|b>\<assign\><around*|(|\<sigma\><rsup|2><rsub|b>|)><rsup|1/2><rsub|b>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|DefnLocalEQFT>A <with|font-series|bold|Wilson\UItô diffusion> is a
    continuous stochastic process <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
    taking values in the set of smooth functions on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>
    with the following properties.

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><with|font-series|bold|Dynamics>. There is an
      <with|font-shape|italic|effective force>
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> and
      <with|font-shape|italic|an effective diffusivity>
      <math|<around*|(|\<sigma\><rsub|a><rsup|2>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
      such that <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
      is a Markovian Itô diffusion

      <\equation>
        <label|EqWIDE>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>=<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>f<rsub|a>\<mathd\>a+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>\<sigma\><rsub|a>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>.
      </equation>

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Locality>. The effective force <math|f>
      and the effective diffusivity <math|\<sigma\><rsup|2>> are local
      observable fields.
    </enumerate-alpha>

    We call equation<nbsp><eqref|EqWIDE> a <with|font-series|bold|Wilson\UItô
    differential equation> (WIDE). A <with|font-series|bold|Wilson\UItô
    field> is the random field <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> obtained as
    the terminal value of a Wilson\UItô diffusion
    <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>.
  </definition>

  The main goal of this paper is to propose the hypothesis that Euclidean
  quantum field theories can be identified with Wilson\UItô fields. This
  provides a new framework, independent of the path-integral formalism, to
  study Euclidean quantum fields.\ 

  <\enumerate-alpha>
    <item>This description emerges from simple and natural assumptions and
    covers in principle much more than those theories that can be reached
    perturbatively from a Gaussian functional integral.

    <item>The continuity of the process <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>>
    with respect to <math|a> gives it the structure of an Itô diffusion. When
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|a>> and <math|C<rsub|a>> are deterministic (hence
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|a>> is constant by the martingale property of
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|a><rsup|2>>), and without any assumption of a
    perturbative regime, the Wilson\UItô random field
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> comes with an associated Gaussian field

    <\equation*>
      X<rsup|C><rsub|a>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2><rsub|b>*\<sigma\><rsub|b>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|b>,
    </equation*>

    and a coupling to it. In that case, <math|X<rsup|C><rsub|\<infty\>>> is a
    white noise

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><around*|[|X<rsup|C><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>X<rsup|C><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|y|)>|]>=\<sigma\><rsup|2>C<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x-y|)>=\<sigma\><rsup|2>\<delta\><around*|(|x-y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Therefore one expects that

    <\equation*>
      \<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|C>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>f<rsub|a>\<mathd\>a
    </equation*>

    is, in general, only a distribution of very low regularity.

    <item>Eq.<nbsp><eqref|EqWIDE> makes sense for fields defined on and/or
    taking values in manifolds or vector bundles and for which the
    path-integral formalism is less clear to apply. Our formalism is
    non-perturbative and trades the use of functional integrals against Itô
    calculus.

    <item>Let <math|A=A<around*|(|a|)>> be a possibly random, adapted,
    increasing change of scale such that <math|A<around*|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|A<around*|(|\<infty\>|)>=\<infty\>>, and let
    <math|\<mathd\><wide|W|~><rsub|a>\<assign\>A<rprime|'><around*|(|a|)><rsup|-1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>>,
    <math|<wide|C|~><rsub|a>=C<rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>>,
    <math|<wide|f|~><rsub|a>\<assign\>f<rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>> and
    <math|<wide|\<sigma\>|~><rsub|a>\<assign\>\<sigma\><rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>>.
    Then we have

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a><wide|C|~><rsub|a>
      <wide|f|~><rsub|a>\<mathd\>a+<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|a><wide|C|~><rsub|a>|)><rsup|1/2>
      <wide|\<sigma\>|~><rsub|a>\<mathd\><wide|W|~><rsub|a>,
    </equation*>

    which shows that Wilson\UItô diffusions are covariant wrt. random changes
    of spatial scales. In particular, this justifies that the diffusion has
    to be averaged with <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>>. Note that
    observables are also covariant: If <math|<around*|(|O<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>>
    is an observable for <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> then
    <math|<around*|(|O<rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>|)><rsub|a>> is an observable
    for the diffusion <math|<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|A<around*|(|a|)>>|)><rsub|a>>.

    <item>Only the law of the terminal value
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> and the averages of observables are the
    physical content of a Wilson\UItô diffusion.
  </enumerate-alpha>

  <with|font-series|bold|Dyson\USchwinger equations and martingale problems>
  \U The law of the process <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
  is determined by a <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|martingale
  problem>>: for all sufficiently nice scale-dependent test functions
  <math|F<around|(|a,\<psi\>|)>>, the process

  <\equation*>
    M<rsup|F><rsub|a>\<assign\>F<around|(|a,\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a><around*|(|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|b>F|)>*<around|(|b,\<varphi\><rsub|b>|)>\<mathd\>b
  </equation*>

  is an <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>>-martingale where

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|b>F<around*|(|b,\<varphi\>|)>>|<cell|\<assign\>>|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|b>F<around*|(|b,\<varphi\>|)>+\<mathD\>F<around*|(|b,\<varphi\>|)>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b>f<rsub|b>
    >>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|+<frac|1|2>Tr
    <around*|[|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>\<sigma\><rsub|b><rsup|2><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>\<mathD\><rsup|2>F<around*|(|b,\<varphi\>|)>|]>>>>>><label|eq:generator>
  </equation>

  is the <with|font-series|bold|generator> of the Wilson\UItô diffusion. Here
  we denote by <math|\<mathD\>\<assign\>\<delta\>/\<delta\>\<varphi\>> the
  gradient of a functional <math|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>F<around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>>
  with respect to the field <math|\<varphi\>> taken wrt. the <math|L<rsup|2>>
  norm and by <math|Tr> the trace operator.

  <section|Forward-backward stochastic differential equations>

  Some observables can be obtained via the conditional expectation of a
  function of <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>, i.e. let
  <math|O<rsub|a>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>>.
  This kind of martingale is called <with|font-shape|italic|closed>, i.e. it
  admits a \Pterminal value\Q <math|O<rsub|\<infty\>>=F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>
  from which it can be reconstructed. In the case when <math|F> is a linear
  functional we have

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|a>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>=F<around*|(|\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>|)>,
  </equation*>

  assuming suitable integrability conditions here and below. Using the
  Wilson\UItô dynamics, and since <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> is a
  martingale, we have

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varphi\><rsub|a>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><big|int><rsub|a><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b>f<rsub|b>*\<mathd\>b=\<varphi\><rsub|a>+<big|int><rsub|a><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b>f<rsub|a>\<mathd\>b>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varphi\><rsub|a>+C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>f<rsub|a>>>>>><label|eq:lin-comp>
  </equation>

  where \ <math|C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>\<assign\>C<rsub|\<infty\>>-C<rsub|a>>. In
  general we are not allowed to form non-linear
  <with|font-shape|italic|local> functions
  <math|F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>> of the distribution
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>: the locality condition for an observable
  is non-trivial and usually requires renormalization. As a consequence, we
  do not expect non-linear local observables to be closed martingales.
  Ignoring for the moment this difficulty, we note that when
  <math|F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>> is well-defined the
  observables <math|<around*|(|O<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> are closed martingales
  and they satisfy a <with|font-shape|italic|backward>
  <with|font-shape|italic|stochastic differential equations>
  (BSDEs)<nbsp><cite|maForwardBackwardStochasticDifferential2007>

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>O<rsub|a>=Z<rsup|O><rsub|a>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>,
  </equation*>

  for a pair of adapted processes <math|<around*|(|O<rsub|a>,Z<rsup|O><rsub|a>|)>>
  with <with|font-shape|italic|terminal> condition
  <math|O<rsub|\<infty\>>=F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>.

  A procedure to construct local observables starts with some approximate
  local observable given by a function <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|a<rsub|0>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>
  localized at scale <math|a<rsub|0><rsup|-1>> and setting

  <\equation*>
    O<rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|a>\<assign\><bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|a<rsub|0>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  One can then study the convergence of the family of the
  <with|font-shape|italic|non-local> observables
  <math|<around|(|O<rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|a>|)><rsub|0\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>a<rsub|0>>>
  as <math|a<rsub|0>\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>. Provided the functions
  <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|a<rsub|0>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>
  contains appropriate (diverging) renormalizations one is able to show that
  the observables <math|O<rsup|a<rsub|0>>> converge to a local observable as
  <math|a<rsub|0>\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>.\ 

  This approach leads naturally to the analysis of a general class of
  <with|font-shape|italic|forward-backward stochastic differential equation>
  (FBSDEs) of the form

  <\equation>
    \<mathd\>\<phi\><rsub|a>=<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><wide|f|~><rsub|a>\<mathd\>a+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><wide|\<sigma\>|~><rsub|a>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|a><label|eq:approx-sde>
  </equation>

  for some approximately local functionals

  <\equation*>
    <wide|f|~><rsub|a>=<bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|F<around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>,<separating-space|1em><wide|\<sigma\>|~><rsub|a>=<bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|\<Sigma\><rsup|2><around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]><rsup|1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Recall <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>> may depend on <math|\<phi\><rsub|a>>
  in a general setting. Note also that we use the letter <math|\<phi\>> for
  these \Papproximate\Q dynamics while we use <math|\<varphi\>> for a local
  dynamics. These FBSDE are not proper Wilson\UItô diffusions, since their
  coefficients are non-local. BSDEs and FBSDEs are well studied in the
  mathematical literature (see e.g.<nbsp><cite|maForwardBackwardStochasticDifferential2007>)
  and we dispose also of numerical methods to approximate their solutions. In
  relation to the numerical aspects, note that the formalism allows us to
  replace <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> by a finite discrete lattice
  <math|<around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<bbb-Z\>\<cap\><around*|[|-L,L|]>|)><rsup|d>>
  with mesh <math|\<varepsilon\>>.

  <subsection|Linear-like force>

  To give a first example consider the case of an approximate force <math|F>
  with a linear component and a constant diffusivity

  <\equation*>
    F<around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=<with|color|red|<with|color|black|\<alpha\>>><around*|(|-A\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+h<around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<Sigma\><around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=<with|color|red|<with|color|black|\<alpha\><rsup|1/2>>>,
  </equation*>

  for some positive constant <math|\<alpha\>>, some positive linear operator
  <math|A> and some additional force component
  <math|h<around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>. For a local operator
  <math|A> the linear functional <math|A\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is always
  well-defined in the space of distributions, so it defines a local force
  field. We assume here that the averaging operators <math|C<rsub|a>> are
  deterministic, field-independent and commute with <math|A>. Think of the
  case where <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>>, <math|h=0> and <math|C<rsub|a>> is
  given by Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:main-averaging>. Using<nbsp><eqref|eq:lin-comp>
  we have

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>=<with|color|black|\<phi\><rsub|a>-\<alpha\>
    C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>A\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>+\<alpha\>
    C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|h<around*|(|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]><rsub|>>
  </equation*>

  Solving for <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>>
  and letting

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\><rsub|a>\<assign\><around*|(|1+\<alpha\>
    C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>A|)><rsup|-1>\<phi\><rsub|a>,
  </equation*>

  we have <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> and

  <\equation>
    \<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|a>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|h<around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]><with|color|black|\<mathd\>a+
    <wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>,<label|eq:Q-eq>
  </equation>

  where <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<assign\><with|color|black|\<partial\><rsub|a>><rsup|><around*|(|A<rsup|-1><around*|(|1+\<alpha\>C<rsub|\<infty\>,a>A|)><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  This computation shows that the linear component in the force can always be
  integrated and gives rise to a modified FBSDE where the local averaging
  operator <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>> has been replaced by the operator
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>>. Note in particular that the Gaussian field

  <\equation>
    X<rsub|a><rsup|Q>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a
    ><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|c><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|c>,<label|eq:GFF>
  </equation>

  has covariance <math|Q<rsub|a><with|color|black|-Q<rsub|0>>> and that
  <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|Q>> has covariance
  <math|\<alpha\>*<around*|(|1+\<alpha\>A|)><rsup|-1>>. One gets back the
  operator <math|A<rsup|-1>> in the large <math|\<alpha\>> limit, in which
  case <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|Q>> is a massive GFF in the model
  situation. We can invert the transformation and go from a FBSDE of the
  form<nbsp><eqref|eq:Q-eq> back to the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:approx-sde>
  noting that

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|\<infty\>,a><rsup|-1>-\<alpha\><rsup|-1>C<rsub|\<infty\>,a><rsup|-1>=A
  </equation*>

  for all <math|a\<geqslant\>0>.

  <subsection|Gradient diffusions>

  Assume further that <math|A> is a symmetric operator and the additional
  force component <math|h> is given by an effective UV-regularized potential
  <math|V<rsub|\<infty\>>> as

  <\equation*>
    h<around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and let

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|a><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>-log
    \<bbb-E\><around*|[|e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<varphi\>+X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|Q>-X<rsub|a><rsup|Q>|)>>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|<around*|(|V<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> is the solution of the
  Polchinski flow equation<nbsp><cite|polchinskiRenormalizationEffectiveLagrangians1984>

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>V<rsub|a>-<frac|1|2>\<mathD\>V<rsub|a><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<mathD\>V<rsub|a>+<frac|1|2><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|a>=0<label|eq:polchinski>
  </equation>

  with terminal condition <math|V<rsub|\<infty\>>> for
  <math|a\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>. Letting
  <math|Z<rsub|a>\<assign\>exp<around*|(|V<rsub|a><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>-V<rsub|0><around*|(|0|)>|)>>
  and using Itô formula, we have that <math|Z<rsub|a>> is a positive
  martingale with <math|Z<rsub|0>=1> and moreover that, under the probability
  measure <math|\<bbb-Q\>> defined on <math|<with|math-font|cal*|F>><math|<rsub|a>>
  by <math|\<mathd\>\<bbb-Q\>\<assign\>Z<rsub|a>\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>>, the
  process <math|\<psi\><rsub|a>> is a martingale with deterministic quadratic
  variation <math|\<mathd\><around*|\<langle\>|\<psi\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|a>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<mathd\>a>,
  so <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is under <math|\<bbb-Q\>> a GFF. Note
  also that it follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> that
  <math|F<rsub|a><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>\<assign\>-\<mathD\>V<rsub|a><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>>
  is a <math|>martingale under <math|\<bbb-Q\>>. Recalling
  Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:GFF>, we conclude that

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-Q\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>e<rsup|V<rsub|0><around*|(|0|)>-V<rsub|a><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|a>|)>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|X<rsub|a><rsup|Q>|)>e<rsup|V<rsub|0><around*|(|0|)>-V<rsub|a><around*|(|X<rsub|a><rsup|Q>|)>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|X<rsub|a><rsup|Q>|)>e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|Q>|)>>|]>|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|Q>|)>>|]>>>>>>><label|eq:gibbs>
  </equation>

  for any function <math|G> and any <math|a\<geqslant\>0>. Here we used that
  <math|e<rsup|-V<rsub|a><around*|(|X<rsup|Q><rsub|a>|)>>> is a martingale.
  This implies that the law <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<infty\>>> of the random field
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is given by

  <\equation>
    \<nu\><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<psi\>|)>=<frac|e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|\<infty\>>><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<psi\>|)>|<big|int>e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|\<infty\>>><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<psi\>|)>><label|eq:gibbs-two>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|\<infty\>>>> denotes the Gaussian of
  covariance <math|Q<rsub|\<infty\>,0>>.

  \;

  This shows that the class of Wilson\UItô fields comprise as a particular
  case the Euclidean quantum fields<nbsp><eqref|eq:gibbs-two> constructed as
  perturbations of a Gaussian field. They are obtained by solving FBSDEs of
  the form

  <\equation>
    \<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|a>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|\<mathD\>*V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>*\<mathd\>a+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>.<label|eq:fbsde>
  </equation>

  which we call <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|Polchinski
  FBSDEs>>, since they describe the Polchinski
  semigroup<nbsp><cite|bauerschmidtLogSobolevInequality2021|bauerschmidtStochasticDynamicsPolchinski2023>.
  Even in this potential framework where there is a formal link with the
  well-known Polchinski flow equation, the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> does
  not require the a priori knowledge of the solution of
  Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski>. Indeed we only used it to derive the formal
  connection while in Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> only the boundary condition
  <math|\<mathD\>*V<rsub|\<infty\>>> is needed.<space|1em>In general this
  quantity needs an ultraviolet regularization that has to be tuned in order
  for the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> to reach a well-defined local limit as
  the regularization is removed.

  <subsection|One-dimensional gradient diffusions>

  Take <math|d=1> and let

  <\equation*>
    F<around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=-<around*|(|-\<Delta\>|)>\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>-\<rho\><around*|(|x|)>v<rprime|'><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, where <math|v<rprime|'>> is the derivative of a
  function <math|v:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> smooth and bounded from
  below and <math|\<rho\>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>> is a
  compactly supported function of the space variable (an IR cutoff). The
  formulation<nbsp><eqref|eq:Q-eq> with <math|A=-\<Delta\>> reads

  <\equation>
    \<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|a>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<rho\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|a>v<rprime|'><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>\<mathd\>a+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>.<label|eq:mild-1d>
  </equation>

  From this equation we see that <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is actually
  comparable in regularity to the <math|d=1> massive GFF
  <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>>> with covariance <math|<around*|(|1+A|)><rsup|-1>>,
  i.e.

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><around*|[|X<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>X<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|y|)>|]>=<frac|1|2>e<rsup|-<around*|\||x-y|\|>>,<space|2em>x,y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,
  </equation*>

  which is a Hölder continuous function for which the mild
  formulation<nbsp><eqref|eq:mild-1d> makes sense. The residual force is
  given by the well-defined potential <math|V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>\<rho\><around*|(|x|)>
  v<around*|(|\<varphi\><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<mathd\>x>.
  By<nbsp><eqref|eq:gibbs> we have

  <\equation>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>=<frac|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around*|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>|]>|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>|]>><label|eq:one-dim-path-integral>
  </equation>

  and this shows that <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is a
  (time-inhomogeneous) Markovian diffusion in the space parameter since
  <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>>> can be described also as the Ornstein\UUhlenbeck
  process, solution of the stochastic differential equation

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>X<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>=-X<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>\<mathd\>x+\<mathd\>B<rsub|x>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around*|(|B<rsub|x>|)><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> is a two-sided
  Brownian motion. Note that this is a Markov process in the space variable.
  Moreover if we take <math|\<rho\>\<rightarrow\>1>, under suitable
  assumptions, the dynamics will converge to a time-homogeneous
  space-Markovian diffusion described by the WIDE (recall that
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>)

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>=<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<bbb-E\><rsub|a><around*|[|-<around*|(|-\<Delta\>|)>\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>-v<rprime|'><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>\<mathd\>a+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>,
  </equation*>

  along the scale variable <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, and, at the same time, it
  is also described by the stochastic differential equation

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>=\<partial\><rsub|\<phi\>>log
    \<Psi\><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<mathd\>x+\<mathd\>B<rsub|x>,
  </equation*>

  along the space variable <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, where
  <math|\<Psi\>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>> is the ground state
  of the Hamiltonian with potential <math|\<phi\><rsup|2>/2+v<around*|(|\<phi\>|)>>
  (see e.g.<nbsp><cite|lorincziFeynmanKactypeTheoremsGibbs2011>).

  In absence of the IR cutoff <math|\<rho\>>, the random field
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is not absolutely continuous wrt.
  <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>>> and therefore the path-integral
  formulation<nbsp><eqref|eq:one-dim-path-integral> loses its meaning while
  the WIDE formulation remains valid.\ 

  This example shows that a local gradient Wilson\UItô dynamics gives rise to
  a Markov process in the space variable. It is natural to conjecture that
  this is a general feature of (a wide class of) higher-dimensional
  Wilson\UItô diffusions.

  <subsection|Variational formulation>

  Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> can be interpreted as the Euler\ULagrange
  equation for a stochastic control problem. To derive this problem we test
  the process

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|a>\<assign\>-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><bbE><rsub|a><around*|[|\<mathD\>*V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>
  </equation*>

  with an adapted test field <math|<around*|(|v<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> and
  integrate both in scale and in the probability space to get the equation

  <\equation>
    \<bbb-E\><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<langle\>|v<rsub|a>,u<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>a+<around*|\<langle\>|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>v<rsub|a>\<mathd\>a,\<mathD\>V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|\<rangle\>>|]>=0<label|eq:weak>
  </equation>

  Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:weak> is the first order condition for the problem of
  minimizing the functional

  <\equation>
    \<Psi\><around*|(|u|)>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><around*|[|V<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<psi\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|u>|)>+<frac|1|2><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<langle\>|u<rsub|a>,u<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>a|]><label|eq:var>
  </equation>

  over all adapted controls <math|<around*|(|u<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>,
  where

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\><rsub|a><rsup|u>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>u<rsub|b>\<mathd\>b+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|a><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|b>,
  </equation*>

  is the controlled process.

  <subsection|Rigorous results>

  Variants of Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>, Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:weak> or of the
  variational problem in Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:var> have been used to construct
  several Euclidean quantum fields including the
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> and <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>
  models<nbsp><cite|barashkov2021variational|barashkov2020variational|barashkov2021phi43>,
  the Høegh-Krohn model<nbsp><cite|barashkov2021variational>, the
  Sine\UGordon model<nbsp><cite|barashkovStochasticControlApproach2022> and
  certain subcritical Euclidean fermionic field
  theories<nbsp><cite|de_vecchi22>. This shows that our approach is
  intrinsically non-perturbative and can be made rigorous. We invite the
  reader to compare this situation with the non-trivial mathematical
  difficulties of the path-integral formalism without cutoffs. As an example,
  take the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> Euclidean quantum field on a torus,
  constructed in<nbsp><cite|barashkov2020variational> via a slightly
  different version of the variational formulation<nbsp><eqref|eq:var>. It is
  known, and proven in<nbsp><cite|barashkov2021phi43>, that the
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> measure is not absolutely continuous with
  respect to the Gaussian free field, so there cannot be a rigorous
  path-integral for it. Similarly, in<nbsp><cite|barashkov2021variational> it
  is shown that some variants of Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:weak> provide effective
  tools to study the infinite volume limit of the
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> and of the
  <math|exp<around*|(|\<Phi\>|)><rsub|2>> Euclidean fields.

  <section|Properties of Wilson\UItô diffusions><label|SectionConstruction>

  <subsection|Coherent germs><label|SectionCoherence>

  Let <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>> be a germ for an observable <math|O>. For
  <math|0\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>a>, one has by Itô's formula

  <\equation*>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|O<rsub|b>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|b><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|b>|)>+R<rsup|O><rsub|b>=<bbE><rsub|b>*<around*|[|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>+R<rsup|O><rsub|a>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|b><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|b>|)>+<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|a><with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>*\<mathd\>c|]>+<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|R<rsup|O><rsub|a>|]>>>>>>
  </equation*>

  where <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c>> is the
  generator<nbsp><eqref|eq:generator> of the Wilson\UItô diffusion. The
  assumption that <math|R<rsup|O>> goes to <math|0> in a strong enough sense
  gives

  <\equation>
    R<rsup|O><rsub|b>=<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><space|-0.1cm><with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>*\<mathd\>c|]>.<label|eq:remO>
  </equation>

  Therefore <math|R<rsup|O>>, and hence <math|O> itself, is completely
  determined by the germ <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>> provided the integral

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|<left|\<\|\|\>|2>|<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>|<right|\<\|\|\>|2>>\<mathd\>c
  </equation*>

  converges absolutely. A germ which has this property is called a
  <with|font-shape|italic|font-series|bold|coherent germ>; its associated
  observable is determined from it. Note that Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:remO> for
  the remainder is equivalent to the BSDE\ 

  <\equation>
    \<mathd\>R<rsup|O><rsub|a>=-<space|-0.1cm><with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>a-Z<rsup|O><rsub|a>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>,<space|1em>R<rsup|O><rsub|\<infty\>>=0<label|eq:bsde-O>
  </equation>

  for the pair of adapted processes <math|<around*|(|R<rsup|O><rsub|a>,Z<rsup|O><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>>.

  \;

  The effective force itself is an observable. For simplicity we assume that
  the diffusivity is taken constant <math|\<sigma\><rsub|a><rsup|2>=1>,
  similar considerations otherwise apply to it. Assume that the force has a
  germ <math|<wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>>
  and a remainder <math|R<rsup|f>> which then, due to<nbsp><eqref|eq:remO>,
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsup|f><rsub|b>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><bbE><rsub|b>*<around*|[|R<rsup|f><rsub|c>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|c>*\<mathD\><wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>*\<mathd\>c>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|+<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L>|\<abovering\>><rsub|c>*<wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>*\<mathd\>c>>>>><label|EqRemainderForceField>
  </equation>

  where we introduced the operator

  <\equation*>
    <wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c>|\<abovering\>>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|c>+<wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|c>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|c>*\<mathD\>+<frac|1|2>*Tr
    <wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|c> \<mathD\><rsup|2><rsub|>.
  </equation*>

  Similarly to the general case of Eq.<nbsp><eqref|eq:bsde-O>,
  Eq.<nbsp><eqref|EqRemainderForceField> gives rise to a BSDEs for the pair
  <math|<around*|(|R<rsup|f><rsub|a>,Z<rsup|f><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> which
  reads

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>R<rsup|f><rsub|a>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L>|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>a-R<rsup|f><rsub|a>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*\<mathD\><wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>a>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|-Z<rsup|f><rsub|a>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>,>>|<row|<cell|R<rsup|O><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>><label|eq:bsde-f>
  </equation>

  A basic requirement for Eq.<nbsp><eqref|EqRemainderForceField> is that the
  source term in<nbsp><eqref|EqRemainderForceField>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:bsde-f> is convergent in the UV, i.e. \ 

  <\equation>
    <label|EqIntegralConditionCoherence><big|int><rsub|a<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|<left|\<\|\|\>|2>|<bbE><rsub|a<rsub|0>><around*|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L>|\<abovering\>><rsub|c>
    <rsub|>*<wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>|<right|\<\|\|\>|2>>\<mathd\>c\<less\>\<infty\>,
  </equation>

  for some scale <math|a<rsub|0>>. If equation<nbsp><eqref|EqRemainderForceField>
  has indeed a unique solution then <math|<wide|f|\<abovering\>>>
  characterizes uniquely the force field <math|f>.

  Assume now we are given an observable <math|O> which has a germ
  <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>>. Under proper assumptions on
  <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>>, and for a choice of
  <math|<wide|f|\<abovering\>>> that ensure an appropriate strong decay of
  <math|R<rsup|f><rsub|a>> as <math|a\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>, the coherence
  relation

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|<left|\||2>|<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>|<right|\||2>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>c\<less\>\<infty\>
  </equation*>

  is a consequence of the relation

  <\equation*>
    <label|EqHatCoherence><big|int><rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|<left|\||2>|<bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|c>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|c><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|c>|)>|]>|<right|\||2>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>c\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  A family <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>>> that satisfies the
  estimate<nbsp><eqref|EqHatCoherence> is called an
  <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|approximately coherent
  germ>>. In those terms, Eq.<nbsp><eqref|EqIntegralConditionCoherence>
  states that <math|<wide|f|\<abovering\>>> is an approximately coherent germ
  for the force field <math|f>.

  These considerations lead to the following strategy for
  <with|font-shape|italic|constructing> the law of a random field
  <math|\<varphi\>>. Associate to each force germ
  <math|<around|(|<wide|f|\<abovering\>><rsub|c>|)><rsub|c>> and each scale
  parameter <math|a<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> the solution to the FBSDEs

  <\equation>
    <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|b>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b><around*|(|<wide|f<rsub|b>|\<abovering\>><rsub|><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|b>|)>+R<rsup|f,a<rsub|0>><rsub|b>|)>\<mathd\>b+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|b><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|R<rsup|f,a<rsub|0>><rsub|b>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|a<rsub|0>><bbE><rsub|b>*<around*|[|R<rsup|f,a<rsub|0>><rsub|c>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|c>\<mathD\><wide|f<rsub|c>|\<abovering\>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|c>|)>|]>\<mathd\>c
    >>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|+<big|int><rsub|b><rsup|a<rsub|0>><bbE><rsub|b><around*|[|<around*|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L>|\<abovering\>><rsub|c>
    *<wide|f<rsub|c>|\<abovering\>>|)><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|a<rsub|0>><rsub|c>|)>|]>*\<mathd\>c>>>>><label|EqCoupledSystemDynamicsRemainder>
  </equation>

  for <math|0\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>a<rsub|0>> with mixed initial/final
  conditions

  <\equation>
    \<varphi\><rsub|0><rsup|a<rsub|0>>=0,<space|2em>R<rsup|f,a<rsub|0>><rsub|a<rsub|0>>=0.
  </equation>

  Now we need to find <math|<around|(|<wide|f<rsub|b>|\<abovering\>><rsub|>|)><rsub|b\<geqslant\>0>>
  such that the coupled system <eqref|EqCoupledSystemDynamicsRemainder> has a
  unique solution for all <math|a<rsub|0>> and <math|a<rsub|0>>-uniform
  estimates that entail sufficient compactness to pass to the limit in
  <eqref|EqCoupledSystemDynamicsRemainder>. In<nbsp><cite|de_vecchi22> this
  strategy has been used to construct some subcritical Gibbs measures on
  Grassmann fields.

  <subsection|Link with factorization algebras>

  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:obs> gives rise only to a vector space
  structure on the set of observables. Since the product of two martingales
  is, generally speaking, not a martingale, we do not have a natural way of
  multiplying observables. However for two observables
  <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> that have some coherent germs
  <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|1>,<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|2>> with
  disjoint supports, say they are at distance strictly larger than
  <math|1/a<rsub|0>>, we can set for <math|a\<geqslant\>a<rsub|0>>

  <\equation*>
    <wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|12|)>><rsub|a>\<assign\><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|1|)>><rsub|a>
    <wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|2|)>><rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|Tr <wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>\<sigma\><rsub|a><rsup|2><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|1|)>><rsub|a>\<mathD\><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|2|)>><rsub|a>=0>,
  for <math|a\<geqslant\>a<rsub|0>>, because of the support condition, one
  has

  <\equation*>
    <wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|12|)>><rsub|a>=<around|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|1|)>><rsub|a>|)><wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|2|)>><rsub|a>+<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|1|)>><rsub|a><around|(|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|2|)>><rsub|a>|)>
  </equation*>

  therefore provided the germs are approximately coherent and we impose
  sufficient decay on <math|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|i|)>><rsub|a>>
  and moderate growth conditions on <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|i|)>><rsub|a>>,
  it is possible to guarantee that <math|<wide|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|a>|\<abovering\>>*<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|12|)>><rsub|a>>
  is integrable and therefore <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsup|<around*|(|12|)>><rsub|a>>
  is itself an approximately coherent germ which defines a unique observable
  <math|O<rsup|<around*|(|12|)>>\<backassign\>O<rsup|<around*|(|1|)>>\<ast\>O<rsup|<around*|(|2|)>>>.
  This gives on a subspace of observables a natural pre-factorization algebra
  structure, as defined in Costello\UGwilliam<nbsp><cite|costelloFactorizationAlgebrasQuantum2017a|costelloFactorizationAlgebrasQuantum2021>.

  <section|Gauge theories><label|SectionGauge>

  In this section we assume that the field <math|\<varphi\>> is a connection
  on a principal bundle over <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, with finite
  dimensional compact structure group <with|font|Euler|G> and Lie algebra
  <math|\<frak-g\>>. Recall that the space of connections is affine with
  <math|\<frak-g\>>-valued <math|1>-forms as underlying vector space<math|>.
  The gauge group <math|<with|font|Euler|>>consists of
  <with|font|Euler|G>-valued functions and acts on connections by
  <math|g\<cdummy\>\<varphi\>=Ad<rsub|g>\<varphi\>-<around*|(|\<mathd\>g|)>
  g<rsup|-1>> and forms by <math|g\<cdummy\>f=Ad<rsub|g>f>. We assume
  moreover that the force field is a gauge covariant function of the
  underlying field <math|f<rsub|a>=f<rsub|a><around*|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>>
  (taking values in the space of <math|1>-forms) :

  <\equation*>
    g\<cdot\>f<rsub|a><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=f<rsub|a>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>\<psi\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  for every <math|g\<in\>\<frak-G\>> and connection <math|\<psi\>>. Given a
  connection <math|\<varphi\>>, denote by
  <math|h<rsup|x*y><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> the <math|\<varphi\>>-holonomy
  along the geodesic from <math|x> to <math|y>. Recall that

  <\equation>
    <label|EqActionOnHolonomy>h<rsup|x*y>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>\<varphi\>|)>=g<around|(|x|)>*h<rsup|x*y><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>*g<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  Let <math|\<chi\><rsub|a><around|(|x,y|)>> be a symmetric function of
  <math|<around|(|x,y|)>>. We define a map
  <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> acting on
  <math|1>-forms by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>*\<omega\>|)><around|(|x|)>\<assign\><frac|1|a<rsup|1/2>>*<big|int>\<chi\><rsub|a><around|(|x,y|)><math-up|Ad><rsub|h<rsup|x*y><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<omega\><around|(|y|)>\<mathd\>y.
  </equation*>

  The operator <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2>> is symmetric and
  <math|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\><around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|)><rsup|2>>
  is symmetric and non-negative. By <eqref|EqActionOnHolonomy>, it is also
  gauge covariant:

  <\equation*>
    g\<cdot\><around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>*\<omega\>|)>=<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>\<varphi\>|)>*<around|(|g\<cdot\>\<omega\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The tangent space of the gauge orbit at a given connection
  <math|\<varphi\>> is spanned by the elements of the form
  <math|\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\>>h>, where <math|\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\>>>
  is the <math|\<varphi\>>-covariant derivative and <math|h> an arbitrary
  (smooth enough) <math|<with|font|Euler|g>>-valued<math|> <math|0>-form,

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\>>h=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>h+<around*|[|\<varphi\><rsub|i>,h|]>|)>\<mathd\>x<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  Two scale-dependent families of connections
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> and
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rprime|'><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> are
  said to be <with|font-shape|italic|gauge equivalent> if there exists a
  scale-dependent family of gauge transforms
  <math|<around|(|g<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> such that
  <math|\<varphi\><rprime|'><rsub|a>=g<rsub|a>\<cdot\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>> for
  all <math|a\<geqslant\>0>. We note that if
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> is a solution
  of the Wilson\UItô equation <eqref|EqWIDE> with
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|a>\<equiv\>1>, and
  <math|<around|(|g<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> is an adapted process
  that is differentiable in <math|a> and takes values in
  <math|C<rsup|1><around|(|M,\<frak-G\>|)>> then
  <math|\<varphi\><rsup|g><rsub|a>\<assign\>g<rsub|a>\<cdot\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>>
  satisfies the equation

  <\equation>
    <label|EqGaugedDynamics><tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsup|g><rsub|a>>|<cell|=>|<cell|g<rsub|a>\<cdummy\><around*|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|a>|)>-\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\><rsub|t><rsup|a>><around*|(|<wide|g|\<dot\>><rsub|t>g<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<mathd\>a|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|g><rsub|a>|)>-\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\><rsub|a><rsup|g>><around|(|<wide|g|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*g<rsup|-1><rsub|a>|)>|)>*\<mathd\>a
    >>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|g><rsub|a>|)>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>.>>>>>
  </equation>

  This is a particular case of a more general situation.

  <\proposition>
    <label|LemGaugeAction>For any adapted process
    <math|<around|(|h<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>> with values in
    <math|C<rsup|1>(M,<with|math-font|Euler|g)>> the solution
    <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|h|)>><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
    to the equation

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|h|)>><rsub|a>=<around*|(|<around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a>|)><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|h|)>><rsub|a>|)>+\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\><rsup|<around*|(|h|)>><rsub|a>>h<rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>a+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|h|)>><rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>
    </equation*>

    is gauge equivalent to the solution of a Wilson\UItô equation

    <\equation>
      \<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsup|<around|[|h|]>><rsub|a>=<around*|(|<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a>|)><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|[|h|]>><rsub|a>|)>*\<mathd\>a+<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a><rsup|1/2><around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|[|h|]>><rsub|a>|)>\<mathd\>W<rsup|h><rsub|a><label|eq:g-transf>
    </equation>

    driven by another Brownian motion <math|W<rsup|h>>. So the law of the
    gauge orbit of <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|0|)>><rsub|a>|)><rsub|a\<geqslant\>0>>
    is well-defined.
  </proposition>

  The proof proceeds by solving the ordinary differential equation
  <math|<wide|g|\<dot\>><rsub|a>*g<rsub|a><rsup|-1>=h<rsub|a>> and remarking
  that <math|\<varphi\><rsup|<around*|[|h|]>><rsub|a>\<assign\>g<rsub|a>\<cdummy\>\<varphi\><rsup|<around|(|h|)>*><rsub|a>>
  solves<nbsp><eqref|eq:g-transf> with <math|\<mathd\>W<rsup|h><rsub|a>:=g<rsub|a>\<cdot\>\<mathd\>W<rsub|a>>,
  which is a Brownian motion by Itô isometry.\ 

  As Parisi\UWu stochastic quantization scheme<nbsp><cite|parisiPerturbationTheoryGauge1981>
  this approach allows to quantize gauge theories without path integrals and
  their associated ghosts or BRS symmetries. Adding terms of the form
  <math|\<mathd\><rsub|\<varphi\><rsub|a>>h<rsub|a>> in the drift allows us
  to perform gauge-fixing in analogy to the Zwanziger\UDeTurck\USadun
  trick<nbsp><cite|damgaardStochasticQuantization1988>. Moreover it points to
  a covariant formulation of the flow equation for the effective force
  <math|f<rsub|a>>

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>f<rsub|a>+f<rsub|a><wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<mathD\>f<rsub|a>+<frac|1|2>Tr
    <wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|a>\<mathD\><rsup|2>f<rsub|a>=0<label|eq:C-polchinski>
  </equation>

  with terminal condition <math|f<rsub|\<infty\>>>. This is similar to
  Polchinski equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> with the difference that the
  cutoff propagator has been replaced by a covariant averaging operator.

  <section|Conclusions>

  We introduced Wilson\UItô fields, a novel class of random fields described
  by \Plocal interactions\Q. Our point of view is inscribed in the general
  idea of <with|font-series|bold|<with|font-shape|italic|stochastic
  quantization>><nbsp><cite|damgaardStochasticQuantization1988> initiated by
  Parisi\UWu<nbsp><cite|parisiPerturbationTheoryGauge1981>, which replaces
  the use of functional integrals with stochastic partial differential
  equations for the Euclidean fields. This topic has recently witnessed a
  renewed interest since SPDEs can be used to study rigorously Euclidean
  fields: see e.g.<nbsp> <cite|chandraLangevinDynamic2D2020|chandraStochasticQuantisationYangMillsHiggs2022|chevyrevInvariantMeasureUniversality2023>
  for non-Abelian gauge theories, <cite|bailleulPhiMeasuresCompact2023|bailleulUniquenessPhiMeasures2023>
  for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on Riemmanian manifolds and
  also<nbsp><cite|GH21> for a partial verification of the
  Osterwalder\USchrader axioms in <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>. However, at
  variance with classical stochastic quantization methods, our approach does
  not require to introduce fictious additional parameters and depends on a
  physically relevant scale of observation, motivated by the Kadanoff\UWilson
  picture of renormalization and by the mathematical framework for Euclidean
  QFT via effective theories, see e.g.<nbsp><cite|costelloRenormalizationEffectiveField2011|salmhoferRenormalizationIntroduction2007|rychkovEPFLLecturesConformal2016>.
  Wilson\UItô fields have a natural intrinsic definition, independent of
  cutoff procedures and it seems interesting to pursue further their study,
  e.g. investigate spatial Markov properties, reflection positivity,
  uniqueness/non-uniqueness of solutions, numerical approximations,
  description of scale-invariant and conformal invariant fields.

